<body>
    <h1>iOS cache and storage</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <div class=3D"contentLayout2">
<style>[data-colorid=3Dzna0e0lcsm]{color:#bf2600} html[data-color-mode=3Dda=
rk] [data-colorid=3Dzna0e0lcsm]{color:#ff6640}</style>
<div class=3D"columnLayout fixed-width" data-layout=3D"fixed-width">
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
<div class=3D"innerCell">
<p>The cache stored by an app can contribute to the overall size of the app=
 on iOS, but the impact on app size is generally minimal.</p>
<p>In iOS, each app is allocated a certain amount of storage space in the d=
evice's file system. This space is shared between the app's executable code=
, its resources, and its data. The amount of space allocated to an app vari=
es depending on the device and the version of iOS, but it typically ranges =
from a few hundred megabytes to several gigabytes.</p>
<p>The size of an app's cache is limited by the amount of storage space all=
ocated to the app. <strong>The iOS system automatically manages the cache a=
nd deletes files that have not been accessed in a while or are no longer ne=
eded. This helps prevent the cache from growing too large and consuming too=
 much storage space.</strong></p>
<p>In general, the size of an app's cache is relatively small compared to t=
he size of its executable code and resources. The cache is typically used t=
o store temporary files, such as downloaded content or images, that can be =
recreated if necessary. The cache is not intended to store large amounts of=
 data that would significantly impact the app's size.</p>
<p>However, it's still important to be mindful of cache usage and ensure th=
at the cache is being used efficiently. You should avoid caching unnecessar=
y data and periodically clear the cache to free up space. Additionally, you=
 can use compression or other techniques to reduce the size of cached data =
and minimize its impact on the app's size.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<div class=3D"columnLayout two-equal" data-layout=3D"two-equal">
<div class=3D"cell normal" data-type=3D"normal">
